{
  "comment": "The operation sequence both drives run, in order, on copies of the same start deck. params is the nested Rails params hash; each drive encodes it the same way (bracketed form keys, or multipart when a step uploads). upload values name a file under fixtures/.",
  "steps": [
    {
      "name": "get-home",
      "mod": "core",
      "method": "get",
      "path": "/",
      "deliberate": {
        "why": "Ruby names each script bundle by its wall clock, so the .js names cannot match the port's fixture clock. The output card has no history (card-mod-assets set/right/asset_output.rb:9-11), so its file takes the stand-in action id Time.now.to_i (card-mod-carrierwave lib/carrier_wave/file_card_uploader.rb:147-151, 202-205, 225-226), and store_output writes it on each update (card-mod-assets set/abstract/asset_outputter.rb:36-51). Card::Assets.refresh runs that cascade on each request of an eager deck (card-mod-assets lib/card/assets.rb:13-27). The port names the bundle by the same rule, floor(clock / 1000) (src/card/actions/style-asset-events.ts, the stamp in updateAssetOutput). The battery drive gives the port a fixture clock that steps one second per read (tools/parity-side-effects/ts-drive.mjs:155-161, 187-193), so its 15 script writes keep 15 names. Ruby's 15 writes land on 12 names: three land in a second an earlier write already took. Every bundle has the same bytes in both engines (one stylesheet hash for the 18 .css, one script hash for the .js), so the only difference is three extra writes of identical script bytes that the wall clock collided in Ruby. No fixed clock reproduces Ruby's collisions; the pins below hold the exact entries.",
